<section id="positioning">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-heading reveal">
            <span class="kicker">Why fleet</span>
            <h2>Parallel agents are easy now. Trusting them isn't.</h2>
            <p>Running agents side by side on git worktrees is the default pattern: Claude Code, Cursor and most agent apps do it. What developers still worry about is the output. In Stack Overflow's 2025 survey, 87% were concerned about AI agents' accuracy and 81% about security and privacy <a class="cmp-src" href="#src-S1">S1</a>. fleet is built around that.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="rule-columns">
            <div class="rule-col reveal">
                <span class="rule-kicker">Permissioned orchestration</span>
                <p>Claude manages your agents under per-project allow, ask and forbid rules, with gates on commit, push and merge. None of the tools we compared document gates on orchestration actions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rule-col reveal">
                <span class="rule-kicker">A process with gates</span>
                <p>Ai-DLC stops at each stage for your approval. None of the tools we compared document a staged process with human gates.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rule-col reveal">
                <span class="rule-kicker">Terminal, many repos, any machine</span>
                <p>One project spans many repos. Its own multiplexer detaches and reattaches, and remote machines open in the same window over ssh. Many alternatives are desktop apps.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="install-note section-note reveal">Free and open source. You bring your own Claude Code plan.</p>
    </div>
</section>
